Subject: [PATCH v3 0/6] lib: scatterlist: fix sg_split() geometry and bounds checks, add KUnit tests

Andrew asked for a respin of v2 with the feedback incorporated. Besides
the automated review of v2, I ran a property-based review of v2 against
the kernel-doc contract of sg_split() (userspace build of lib/sg_split.c,
both with and without CONFIG_NEED_SG_DMA_LENGTH, plain and ASan/UBSan,
plus a differential test against v2 itself). It found that v2's
zero-nents fix was incomplete, two pre-existing problems v2 didn't touch,
and several inaccuracies in v2's commit messages. v3 addresses all of
them; the behaviour changes for callers are listed below so they can be
judged on their own.

sg_split() is EXPORT_SYMBOL'd, so its contract is "any caller, any
split_sizes[]", not just the in-tree call sites. None of the bugs below is
triggered by an in-tree caller today as far as I can tell -- all are
latent.

The bugs (all Fixes: f8bcbe62acd0):

1. sg_calculate_split() nb_splits overshoot (unchanged code since v1).
   Partial coverage ending mid-entry of a non-last input entry
   overshoots the counter to -1, misses the !nb_splits termination check
   and folds every remaining input entry into the last split. In the
   mapped pass with coalesced DMA segments the same overshoot is a heap
   overflow: the DMA pass counts more entries than were allocated.

2. sg_split_phys() ->length clobber (unchanged code since v2). On
   !NEED_SG_DMA_LENGTH configs sg_dma_len() aliases ->length, so the
   DMA-scrub line zeroes the CPU length; non-last entries of a
   multi-entry split keep length 0 for unmapped input.

3. Splits that start at the end of the input (reworked). A zero-size
   split there gets no entry, out_sg is ZERO_SIZE_PTR and the copiers
   write out_sg[-1]; and once that happens, the splits after it are
   never checked, so a request running past the end of the input is
   accepted. v3 rejects the latter with -EINVAL and gives a zero-size
   split at the end of the input one zero-length, end-marked entry.

4. Zero-length first entry (new). A split that starts exactly at the
   end of an input entry (skip on an entry boundary) or at an empty
   entry begins with a zero-length entry; mapped, its first
   sg_dma_len() is 0, where a DMA consumer may stop.

5. Unchecked arguments (new). nb_splits == 0 is an out-of-bounds access
   on the zero-size splitters array, a negative nb_splits fails as
   -ENOMEM, and a negative skip exposes bytes before the input on 64-bit
   (on 32-bit the off_t/unsigned int comparison already makes it
   -EINVAL).

Behaviour changes for callers (only for the inputs described above):

 - a request where skip + sum(split_sizes) exceeds the input now always
   fails with -EINVAL (before, some shapes returned 0 -- or, before v2,
   crashed);
 - a zero-size split at the very end of the input returns a one-entry,
   zero-length, end-marked list with out_mapped_nents[k] == 1 (v2 made
   that out[k] == ZERO_SIZE_PTR with out_mapped_nents[k] == 0);
 - a split that started with a zero-length entry now has one entry fewer
   (and out_mapped_nents[k] one less);
 - nb_splits <= 0 and skip < 0 fail with -EINVAL.
Every other input gives exactly the same result as v2 (checked by a
differential test over the inputs not listed above).

Patch layout (bisect-safe: each step only changes the inputs it
describes, and the suite comes last, after every fix it checks):

  1/6  overshoot fix              -- code unchanged, message corrected
  2/6  ->length clobber fix       -- code unchanged, message corrected
  3/6  end-of-input splits        -- replaces v2 4/5
  4/6  zero-length first entry    -- new
  5/6  argument checks            -- new
  6/6  KUnit suite                -- v2 3/5 + 5/5, extended

Testing.
