expected: UML 22 passed / 3 skipped; x86_64 + KASAN 25/25.]
In userspace (lib/sg_split.c built against stubs, both NEED and !NEED,
plain and ASan/UBSan) the KUnit suite, compiled through a small KUnit
shim, passes; reverting any one fix makes the cases listed in 6/6 fail.
Property-based tests check every split's bytes (CPU and DMA view), end
markers, entry counts, rejection of every past-the-end request and of
invalid arguments, error paths (-ENOMEM at every allocation: no leak,
out[] untouched), on random inputs with up to 48 entries in up to four
chained tables, up to 16 splits, empty entries and entries larger than a
page. Mapped inputs group consecutive entries into DMA segments in any
way an in-tree map_sg can (merged, zero-length entries dropped or kept),
over a contiguous IOVA buffer. Separate tests on fake page addresses
cover entry lengths up to UINT_MAX, totals past 4 GiB and extreme skip /
split_sizes values, and compare a 64-bit and a 32-bit (i386) build of
the same lib/sg_split.c on the same inputs. Every mutant of lib/sg_split.c
the tool generated that changes behaviour makes the suite fail. A real
dma_map_sg / IOMMU rig is still not part of this; the mapped coverage is
synthetic.

